=== AGENTS.md ===
# AI Agent Instructions

A Rust backend generated by [Puerto](https://github.com/Guuri11/Puerto): DDD + Hexagonal
(Ports & Adapters), three crates, REST API with poem-openapi. This file is the entry point for any
coding agent (`CLAUDE.md` imports it). Each crate has its own detail:
`business/AGENTS.md`, `infrastructure/AGENTS.md`, `presentation/AGENTS.md`.

## Architecture

```
presentation → infrastructure → business (application → domain)
```

Dependencies point inward only. `business` depends on no other crate of this workspace;
`infrastructure` never depends on `presentation`.

| Crate | Holds | Never |
|---|---|---|
| `business` | domain (models, value objects, errors, repository ports, use case traits) + application (use case impls) | imports a database driver, HTTP framework or adapter |
| `infrastructure` | adapters implementing the domain ports (in-memory or Postgres repositories) | contains business rules |
| `presentation` | HTTP: routes, DTOs, responses, error mapping, health probes, DI wiring | exposes a domain model in a response |

**The Puerto runtime.** What every Puerto project shares is not copied into it: it is a crate,
declared once in the root `Cargo.toml` (`[workspace.dependencies]`, on the same minor as the
`puerto` CLI) and inherited by each member with `{ workspace = true }`.

| Crate | Owns | Imported as |
|---|---|---|
| `puerto-core` | `RepositoryError` (what every repository port fails with), `PageRequest` / `Page<T>` (paged lists, limit capped at 100) | `use puerto_core::{Page, PageRequest, RepositoryError};` |
| `puerto-web` | the server (`App`: tracing, CORS, graceful shutdown), `Config` (read from the environment and `.env`), the `/health` and `/health/ready` probes, `ErrorResponse` | `presentation` only — `main.rs`, `generated/bootstrap.rs`, `use puerto_web::ErrorResponse;` |
| `puerto-sqlx` (`--db` projects) | the Postgres pool, migrations at startup, the readiness `ping`, `map_sqlx_error` (driver error → `RepositoryError`) | `infrastructure` only — re-exported by `infrastructure/src/db.rs`, which keeps the project's `MIGRATOR` |

`puerto-core` depends on nothing but `thiserror`, so the domain may use it; `puerto-web` carries
poem and stays in `presentation`; `puerto-sqlx` carries sqlx and stays in `infrastructure`. Do not
copy their code into the project; upgrade them with the `puerto` CLI (`puerto validate` warns when
they drift apart). Your own middleware and routes go in `presentation/src/app.rs`, which no
generator touches.

**Modules are declared inline in each crate's `lib.rs`** (nested `pub mod {}` blocks). There are
no `mod.rs` files: adding a use case is one line in `lib.rs` plus its file.

## `puerto.toml` is the source of truth

It describes every entity, its fields and use cases. Generate code from it instead of writing the
wiring by hand:

```bash
puerto generate scaffold <Name> -- title:String price:i64!    # every layer of a CRUD entity
puerto generate use-case <Entity> <action>                    # one more use case (snake_case)
puerto generate value-object <Name> <type>                    # a shared value object
puerto generate migration <name>                              # SQL migration (--db projects)
puerto generate bootstrap                                     # rewire DI from puerto.toml
puerto validate                                               # check puerto.toml against the code
```

- **Never hand-edit `presentation/src/generated/bootstrap.rs`** — it is rewritten from
  `puerto.toml`. Change the manifest, then run `puerto generate bootstrap`.
- `puerto generate scaffold` refuses to overwrite an entity that already has files; `--force`
  discards hand-written changes in them.

## Frontend (`web/`)

A React + daisyUI app on Bun that talks to the API under `/api`. It follows the same Dependency
Rule as the crates (`domain` ← `application` ← `infrastructure` ← `presentation`), checked by
`make web/lint`. Its rules — layers, generated files, where use cases go — are in `web/AGENTS.md`.

The OpenAPI spec is the contract between the two halves: `docs/api/openapi.json`, exported by a
test in `bootstrap.rs`. **After a scaffold and after touching a route or a DTO, run
`make web/sync`** (it re-exports the spec, regenerates the frontend's wire types and formats
`web/`) and commit both. Until then `make test` fails — that failure is the drift check.
`puerto generate scaffold` writes a CRUD entity's screens too; `puerto generate web <Name>` writes
them alone.

## Auth (`[project] auth`)

The API is an OAuth2 resource server (ADR-0021 of Puerto): it verifies the bearer token of every
protected request against the provider at `AUTH_ISSUER` — Keycloak in development
(`make docker-compose/up`, `make auth/ui`), Auth0 or any OpenID Connect provider in production.

- A handler that takes `auth: BearerAuth` is protected: no or a bad token is answered
  `401 auth.unauthenticated` before it runs. `#[require_role("admin")]` on it adds the
  `403 auth.forbidden` check. Both come from `puerto_web`.
- **Roles come from the token only** (`AUTH_ROLES_CLAIM`); the `users.roles` column is not read.
- Every caller is a `User` (`business/src/domain/user/`), created on their first request by
  `infrastructure/src/user/provisioning.rs`. Point at a person with a `UserId`, never with the
  provider's subject. These files are yours.
- `GET /me` answers the caller as the API resolved them. The frontend reads it, never the token.
- A rule that depends on data ("only the owner edits") belongs in the use case, not in an
  attribute.

## Conventions

- **TDD.** Write the failing test first, then the minimum code. Test names:
  `should_<expected_behaviour>_when_<scenario>`, with `// Arrange` / `// Act` / `// Assert`.
- **Errors are machine-readable codes** (`product.not_found`,
  `product.validation_error.title_empty`), never sentences. The API returns them in
  `ErrorResponse.name`; domain validation failures are `422`.
- **Logging:** `tracing::info!` / `warn!` / `error!` directly — no injected logger. A use case logs
  `<use_case>: start` and `<use_case>: done`; never log request payloads (they are user data).
- **No `unwrap()` / `expect()`** outside tests.
- **API First:** change `routes.rs` + `dto.rs` (the contract) before the implementation.

## Check before you are done

```bash
make test      # unit + integration tests
make lint      # clippy -D warnings — the toolchain is pinned in rust-toolchain.toml
make format    # rustfmt check
```

`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the Rust version: clippy gains lints between releases, so a pinned
toolchain is what makes `make lint` give the same answer on every machine.

## How to work here

**Make one commit per piece.** Finish one use case (or one fix), get `make test` and `make lint`
green, commit, then start the next. A rule like "small steps" that is not tied to anything
checkable does not get followed; a commit per piece is visible in `git log`.

**Where a human must read the diff** — calibrate by how forgiving the system is when the code is
wrong:

- **Tier 1 — fails silently, slowly or expensively.** A green test suite is not enough; a person
  reads the whole diff before it is committed: SQL migrations (above all destructive ones),
  anything touching money or other arithmetic the business relies on, authentication and
  authorization checks, uniqueness / deduplication rules, data that crosses a tenant or customer
  boundary.
- **Tier 2 — fails loudly and reversibly.** The checks above are enough: DTO ↔ domain mapping,
  adapters covered by real integration tests, read-only queries, docs.

A change touching both tiers is Tier 1.

**Write decisions down.** When a design decision rests on context that is not in the code, record
it — an ADR under `docs/adr/` or an update to these files — so the next session inherits the
reason instead of re-deriving it or undoing it.

=== Cargo.toml ===
[workspace]
members = ["business", "infrastructure", "presentation"]
resolver = "3"

# The Puerto runtime (ADR-0017 of Puerto): what every Puerto project shares lives in these crates,
# on the same minor as the `puerto` CLI that generated this project.
[workspace.dependencies]
puerto-core = "0.18"
puerto-web = "0.18"
puerto-auth = "0.18"

=== Makefile ===
.PHONY: help setup run \
        test test/domain test/application test/infrastructure \
        lint format format/fix check clean \
        audit audit/fix \
        generate/entity generate/use-case generate/bootstrap \
        web/install web/require-install web/dev web/test web/lint web/sync

CARGO := cargo
BUN   := bun

# The escape byte itself, not `\033`: `echo` does not interpret escapes where `sh` is bash.
GREEN  := $(shell printf '\033[1;32m')
YELLOW := $(shell printf '\033[1;33m')
CYAN   := $(shell printf '\033[1;36m')
RED    := $(shell printf '\033[1;31m')
NC     := $(shell printf '\033[0m')

help: ## Show available commands
	@echo ""
	@echo "${CYAN}Available targets:${NC}"
	@echo ""
	@grep -E '^[a-zA-Z/_-]+:.*?## .*$$' $(MAKEFILE_LIST) | awk 'BEGIN {FS = ":.*?## "}; {printf "  ${YELLOW}%-32s${NC} %s\n", $$1, $$2}'
	@echo ""

setup: ## Install required dev tools (run once after cloning)
	@echo "${CYAN}Setting up development environment...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) install cargo-nextest --locked
	$(CARGO) install cargo-audit --locked

run: ## Run the API server (http://localhost:8080)
	@echo "${GREEN}Starting API server (in memory: data is lost on restart)...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) run -p presentation

test: ## Run all tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running all tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run --workspace
	@if [ -d web ]; then $(MAKE) web/test; fi

test/domain: ## Run domain layer tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running domain tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run -p business -E 'test(domain)'

test/application: ## Run application layer tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running application layer tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run -p business -E 'test(application)'

test/infrastructure: ## Run infrastructure layer tests (requires DB)
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running infrastructure tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run -p infrastructure

lint: ## Clippy with -D warnings
	@echo "${CYAN}Linting code...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) clippy --workspace -- -D warnings
	@if [ -d web ]; then $(MAKE) web/lint; fi

format: ## Check code formatting
	@echo "${CYAN}Checking code formatting...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) fmt --all -- --check

format/fix: ## Fix code formatting
	@echo "${CYAN}Fixing code formatting...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) fmt --all

check: ## Fast compile check (no binary)
	@echo "${CYAN}Checking code...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) check --workspace

clean: ## Remove build artifacts
	@echo "${RED}Cleaning build artifacts...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) clean

audit: ## Run security audit on dependencies
	@echo "${CYAN}Running security audit...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) audit

audit/fix: ## Preview security fixes (dry-run)
	@echo "${CYAN}Previewing security fixes (dry-run)...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) audit fix --dry-run

generate/entity: ## Scaffold a new DDD entity — make generate/entity NAME=Product
	@if [ -z "$$NAME" ]; then \
		echo "${RED}Error: provide entity name — make generate/entity NAME=Product${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi
	@echo "${GREEN}Scaffolding entity '$$NAME'...${NC}"
	puerto generate scaffold $$NAME

generate/use-case: ## Add a use case to an entity — make generate/use-case ENTITY=Product ACTION=delete_product
	@if [ -z "$$ENTITY" ] || [ -z "$$ACTION" ]; then \
		echo "${RED}Error: provide entity and action — make generate/use-case ENTITY=Product ACTION=delete_product${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi
	@echo "${GREEN}Adding use case '$$ACTION' to '$$ENTITY'...${NC}"
	puerto generate use-case $$ENTITY $$ACTION

generate/bootstrap: ## Regenerate bootstrap.rs from puerto.toml
	@echo "${GREEN}Regenerating bootstrap.rs...${NC}"
	puerto generate bootstrap

web/install: ## Install the frontend dependencies (needs Bun)
	@echo "${CYAN}Installing frontend dependencies...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) install

# Not in `make help`: the prerequisite of every target that runs the frontend's tools.
web/require-install:
	@if [ ! -d web/node_modules ]; then \
		echo "${RED}web/node_modules is missing — run: make web/install${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi

web/dev: web/require-install ## Run the frontend dev server (http://localhost:5173, /api proxied to :8080)
	@echo "${GREEN}Starting frontend dev server...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run dev

web/test: web/require-install ## Run the frontend tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running frontend tests...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) test

web/lint: web/require-install ## Typecheck, lint and check the frontend Dependency Rule
	@echo "${CYAN}Linting frontend...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run typecheck && $(BUN) run lint && $(BUN) run lint:arch

web/sync: web/require-install ## After a scaffold or a DTO change: export the OpenAPI spec, regenerate the web types
	@echo "${CYAN}Exporting docs/api/openapi.json...${NC}"
	UPDATE_OPENAPI=1 $(CARGO) test -p presentation openapi
	@echo "${CYAN}Generating web/src/infrastructure/http/schema.d.ts...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run openapi:types
	@echo "${CYAN}Formatting web/ (Biome, no lint fixes)...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run format

.PHONY: docker-compose/up docker-compose/down

DOCKER_COMPOSE := docker compose

docker-compose/up: ## Start the containers
	@echo "${CYAN}Starting containers...${NC}"
	$(DOCKER_COMPOSE) up -d --wait

docker-compose/down: ## Stop the containers
	@echo "${CYAN}Stopping containers...${NC}"
	$(DOCKER_COMPOSE) down

# The template's `run` keeps its recipe; here it gains the provider `bootstrap.rs` discovers.
run: docker-compose/up

.PHONY: auth/ui

auth/ui: docker-compose/up ## The Keycloak console: users, roles, sessions
	@echo "${GREEN}Keycloak: http://localhost:8180/admin/master/console/#/snapshot (admin / admin)${NC}"
	@echo "${GREEN}Sign in to the app as admin@snapshot.localhost / admin-dev-only (role admin)${NC}"

=== business/src/domain/user/errors.rs ===
use puerto_core::RepositoryError;
use thiserror::Error;

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum UserError {
    #[error("user.not_found")]
    NotFound,
    #[error("user.duplicate")]
    Duplicate,
    #[error("user.repository_error")]
    RepositoryError,
}

impl From<RepositoryError> for UserError {
    /// `RepositoryError` is `#[non_exhaustive]`: one a later version adds is the store's problem
    /// (500) until it gets a meaning here.
    fn from(err: RepositoryError) -> Self {
        match err {
            RepositoryError::Duplicate => Self::Duplicate,
            RepositoryError::InvalidData | RepositoryError::Unavailable => Self::RepositoryError,
            _ => Self::RepositoryError,
        }
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn should_convert_a_duplicate_into_the_user_duplicate() {
        let err = UserError::from(RepositoryError::Duplicate);
        assert_eq!(err.to_string(), "user.duplicate");
    }

    #[test]
    fn should_convert_an_unavailable_store_into_a_repository_error() {
        let err = UserError::from(RepositoryError::Unavailable);
        assert_eq!(err.to_string(), "user.repository_error");
    }
}

=== business/src/domain/user/model.rs ===
//! Yours: `puerto new --auth` writes this file once. Every authenticated caller is a `User` row
//! (ADR-0021 § 3 of Puerto): what other entities point at (`owner: UserId`), whichever provider
//! signed the caller in.

use chrono::{DateTime, Utc};
use uuid::Uuid;

/// The id of every `User`: a `Uuid` that cannot be passed where another entity's id is expected.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub struct UserId(Uuid);

impl UserId {
    /// A new, random id — for a user being created.
    pub fn generate() -> Self {
        Self(Uuid::new_v4())
    }

    /// An id read from the outside (a path parameter, a row) — no validation: any `Uuid` is an id.
    pub fn from_uuid(value: Uuid) -> Self {
        Self(value)
    }

    pub fn value(&self) -> Uuid {
        self.0
    }
}

impl std::fmt::Display for UserId {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        self.0.fmt(f)
    }
}

/// Who signed in, as this project knows them. `(issuer, subject)` is the provider's name for the
/// person and is unique; `email` and `name` are what the provider said the first time.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub struct User {
    pub id: UserId,
    pub issuer: String,
    pub subject: String,
    pub email: Option<String>,
    pub name: Option<String>,
    /// Not read in an OIDC project: there the roles come from the token only, so one place
    /// decides.
    pub roles: Vec<String>,
    pub created_at: DateTime<Utc>,
    pub updated_at: DateTime<Utc>,
}

impl User {
    /// The user a first request from `(issuer, subject)` creates (just-in-time provisioning).
    pub fn provision(
        issuer: impl Into<String>,
        subject: impl Into<String>,
        email: Option<String>,
        name: Option<String>,
    ) -> Self {
        let now = Utc::now();
        Self {
            id: UserId::generate(),
            issuer: issuer.into(),
            subject: subject.into(),
            email,
            name,
            roles: vec![],
            created_at: now,
            updated_at: now,
        }
    }

    /// Reconstructs from persistence — no validation. ONLY infrastructure uses this.
    pub fn from_repository(data: User) -> Self {
        data
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn should_hold_what_the_provider_said_when_provisioned() {
        // Act
        let user = User::provision(
            "http://localhost:8180/realms/shop",
            "f81d4fae",
            Some("ada@example.com".into()),
            Some("Ada Lovelace".into()),
        );

        // Assert
        assert_eq!(user.issuer, "http://localhost:8180/realms/shop");
        assert_eq!(user.subject, "f81d4fae");
        assert_eq!(user.email.as_deref(), Some("ada@example.com"));
        assert_eq!(user.name.as_deref(), Some("Ada Lovelace"));
        assert!(user.roles.is_empty());
        assert_eq!(user.created_at, user.updated_at);
    }

    #[test]
    fn should_get_a_new_id_when_two_users_are_provisioned() {
        // Act
        let ada = User::provision("issuer", "ada", None, None);
        let bob = User::provision("issuer", "bob", None, None);

        // Assert
        assert_ne!(ada.id, bob.id);
    }
}

=== business/src/domain/user/repository.rs ===
use async_trait::async_trait;
use puerto_core::RepositoryError;

use super::model::{User, UserId};

#[async_trait]
pub trait UserRepositoryTrait: Send + Sync {
    /// The user the provider `issuer` calls `subject`.
    async fn find_by_subject(
        &self,
        issuer: &str,
        subject: &str,
    ) -> Result<Option<User>, RepositoryError>;
    async fn find_by_id(&self, id: UserId) -> Result<Option<User>, RepositoryError>;
    /// Inserts or updates by id; another user with the same `(issuer, subject)` is `Duplicate`.
    async fn save(&self, user: &User) -> Result<(), RepositoryError>;
}

#[cfg(any(test, feature = "test-utils"))]
pub mod mocks {
    use mockall::mock;

    use super::*;

    mock! {
        pub UserRepository {}

        #[async_trait]
        impl UserRepositoryTrait for UserRepository {
            async fn find_by_subject(
                &self,
                issuer: &str,
                subject: &str,
            ) -> Result<Option<User>, RepositoryError>;
            async fn find_by_id(&self, id: UserId) -> Result<Option<User>, RepositoryError>;
            async fn save(&self, user: &User) -> Result<(), RepositoryError>;
        }
    }
}

